analysis:
This PR changes documentation only; no Swift source was supplied.

model-registry-format.md head 119-122 states that on macOS each file read drops its pages from the file cache (PageCache.swift). This is a memory and availability note. It does not change the aggregate hash or the weight_hash. Base lines 114-119 are unchanged.

configuration.md head 579-590 says verification reads first through an F_NOCACHE descriptor, then calls PageCache.drop. Lines 589-590 say a managed GLM load publishes its own per-file SHA-256 verification instead of bracketing pre/post-load passes. Whether that hash covers the exact bytes loaded is unverified, which is relevant to T-012, T-027 and A-004. The SSD-cache rows (head 615-647) match T-041: TTL at most 1800 s, disk budget and the DEBUG-only key file. Units 8 and 9 show no visible weakening of those rows.

Integration concern, from the file_changes patch only: head line 371 adds DARKBLOOM_VERIFIED_WEIGHTS_FILE (~/.darkbloom/verified-weights.json, VerifiedWeightHashStore.swift). Startup registration reuses a matching record, bound to device, inode, size, mtime and ctime, instead of re-hashing. Under TB-003 the operator (ADV-001) owns this directory. The operator could forge or retain a record, or replace contents while keeping the stat fields, by restoring mtime or reusing an inode on another device. The provider would then advertise a catalog weight_hash without hashing the actual bytes. That would weaken T-012 and T-027 and make the self-reported weight identity depend on operator-writable state.

That line is not visible in my primary units, so I cannot cite it as a finding. The next pass must review:
- whether VerifiedWeightHashStore records are authenticated (MAC or SE key) or trusted plaintext;
- whether ctime and cross-device binding are enforced;
- whether the GLM per-file publication hashes the exact loaded bytes;
- whether the threat model (T-012, A-004) needs updating.
